        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Does a Mini Split Actually Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The common industry range for ductless mini splits is roughly 12 to 20 years, and it overlaps with the figure the Department of Energy gives for heat pumps generally, which is around 15 years. Some well-built systems in mild climates run past twenty. Some poorly installed ones are in trouble within five. The spread is wider than for most appliances because so much depends on work done in the field, not in the factory.
          </p>

          <p>
            A mini split is a heat pump. It moves heat into the house in winter and out in summer, and in many homes it runs most of the year. A central air conditioner in a northern climate might run a few hundred hours a season and then rest. A mini split used as the primary heat source in the same house runs through the winter too. More operating hours means more wear on the compressor, fans and electronics, so a mini split used year-round should not be expected to outlast a summer-only system just because it uses an inverter.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Lifespan in Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">12-20 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical mini split range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~15 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Reasonable expectation</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-4 wks</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common filter cleaning interval</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5-12 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical warranty spread</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The indoor and outdoor units do not always age together. Indoor heads are protected from weather but collect dust, pet hair and moisture, and can grow mold if they are not cleaned. Outdoor units face sun, rain, snow, salt and lawn debris. In many systems the outdoor unit, with the compressor and the main inverter board, is the part whose failure decides whether the system is replaced.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer is this: plan on about 15 years, treat 20 as a good outcome you earn with maintenance, and understand that a bad install or a seaside location can cut that substantially. The sections below explain where those years go and what you can do about each.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Does Installation Quality Really Matter That Much?
          </h2>

          <p>
            More than anything else on this page. A mini split is a sealed refrigerant system, and the installer opens and closes that system on site. If the line set is not flared and torqued correctly, if the system is not pressure tested with dry nitrogen, or if it is not evacuated to a deep vacuum before the refrigerant is released, the system can leak slowly or carry moisture and air inside. Moisture in a refrigerant circuit can form acids and sludge that damage the compressor over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flare connections are the most common leak point on ductless systems. A properly made flare tightened with a torque wrench to the manufacturer's specification seals reliably for years. An overtightened flare can crack and an undertightened one can weep. Leaks may not show up for months, and by then the system has been running short of charge, which makes the compressor run hotter and harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement matters too. Outdoor units should sit level, on a stand or wall bracket that keeps them above snow lines and splashing rain, with clearance around the coil as the manual specifies. Condensate from the indoor head needs a proper downhill drain path. Line set insulation should be intact and protected from sunlight, because UV breaks down exposed foam insulation over a few years and sweating, uninsulated lines waste energy and drip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sizing is the last install factor. An oversized system short-cycles, which is harder on components and worse at controlling humidity. An undersized one runs flat out in extreme weather. Inverter-driven systems tolerate some mismatch better than single-speed equipment, but a proper load calculation still pays off. If you are comparing models before choosing an installer, our <a href="/best-mini-split-heat-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best mini split heat pumps</a> guide covers which systems suit which homes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Usually Breaks First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inverter control boards are a frequent failure on modern mini splits. The inverter is what lets the compressor change speed instead of switching on and off, and it is a large part of why these systems are efficient and quiet. It is also a fairly complex piece of power electronics sitting outdoors, exposed to heat, humidity and electrical surges. Board failures often show up as error codes, a unit that will not start, or a compressor that runs erratically.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sensors and fans come next. Mini splits rely on thermistors, small temperature sensors on the coils, pipes and air intake, to manage the inverter, defrost and fan speeds. A failed thermistor can produce error codes or strange behavior that looks like a bigger problem but is a relatively cheap part. Indoor blower motors and outdoor fan motors also wear, and an indoor blower wheel caked with dust and mold works harder and moves less air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Refrigerant leaks are the slow killer. A mini split should never need routine top-ups. If yours is low on refrigerant, there is a leak, usually at a flare joint, sometimes in a coil corroded by salt air or household chemicals. Topping up without finding and fixing the leak only buys time, and running low on charge stresses the compressor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The compressor is the expensive end of the list. Compressors are generally robust, and when they fail early it is often because of something else: a leak, moisture contamination from installation, repeated electrical problems or a failed board that drove the compressor badly. A compressor failure out of warranty on an older system is usually the point at which replacement makes more sense than repair.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
