        <div id="coastal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Does Coastal Air or Climate Shorten It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Salt air is the biggest environmental threat to an outdoor unit. Airborne salt settles on the thin aluminum fins and copper tubing of the outdoor coil, and with moisture it drives corrosion, including galvanic corrosion where the two metals meet. Over time the fins crumble, the coil loses its ability to move heat, and eventually the tubing can pit through and leak. Cabinets, fasteners and electrical connections suffer too.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much life a coastal system loses depends on distance from the water, wind direction, spray exposure and maintenance. A unit right on the shore in the path of sea spray can deteriorate several times faster than the same unit inland. Many manufacturers offer outdoor units with factory corrosion-resistant coil coatings for coastal installs, and some have specific installation guidance for sites near salt water. If you live near the ocean, ask about these options before buying, not after.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coastal owners can help with simple habits. Rinse the outdoor coil gently with fresh water from a garden hose a few times a year, especially after storms, never with a pressure washer. Place the unit on the side of the house sheltered from the prevailing sea breeze if possible. Inspect for white powdery corrosion on the fins and rust on screws and brackets, and address them early.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold climates bring different stress. In winter, the outdoor coil frosts and the system runs defrost cycles. That is normal, but drainage under the unit matters, because meltwater that refreezes can build ice that damages the coil and fan. A stand that lifts the unit above the snow line and a clear path for defrost water both help. Hot, humid climates mean long cooling seasons and heavy condensate loads, which makes drain line maintenance and indoor coil cleaning more important.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Maintenance Actually Extends Its Life?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the indoor filters. Mini split heads have washable mesh filters, and many manufacturers suggest cleaning them every two to four weeks during heavy use. Lift the front panel, slide out the filters, rinse or vacuum them, let them dry completely and slide them back. Clogged filters reduce airflow, which makes the system work harder, can cause coil icing in cooling mode, and lets dust reach the coil and blower wheel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get the indoor units deep cleaned periodically. Filters do not catch everything, and over time dust and moisture can build up on the indoor coil and the drum-shaped blower wheel, sometimes growing mold that produces a musty smell when the system starts. A professional cleaning, often done with a drain bag around the head and a low-pressure rinse, restores airflow and removes the growth. How often depends on use, pets and humidity; many owners do it every year or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the outdoor unit clear. Trim plants back, clear leaves, grass clippings and snow, and keep the coil clean so air can move freely. Check that the line set insulation is intact and that any line cover is secure. Make sure the condensate drain from each indoor head is flowing; a slow or blocked drain can cause water to drip from the head onto walls and floors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the electronics and watch for early signs. Because the inverter board is a common failure, many installers recommend surge protection, and a <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whole-house surge protector</a> covers the mini split along with everything else on your panel. Pay attention to new noises, weaker output, error codes or rising energy bills. In humid basements and lower floors, pairing the system with a dedicated dehumidifier can reduce the load on it; our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best basement dehumidifiers</a> covers that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Do Mini Split Warranties Actually Cover?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most major brands offer a standard warranty in the range of roughly five years on parts and seven years on the compressor, and many extend that to around ten or twelve years when the system is installed by an authorized or certified contractor and registered within a set window after installation. The exact terms vary by brand and sometimes by region, so read the specific warranty document for the model you are buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labor is usually not covered beyond a short initial period. A parts warranty that covers a compressor or inverter board is valuable, but the labor to recover refrigerant, swap the part, pressure test, evacuate and recharge the system can be substantial. Some contractors offer their own labor warranties; ask what is included and for how long before you sign.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">REGISTER IT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">An unregistered system can lose years of coverage.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Many brands require registration within a set number of days after installation to activate their longer warranty. Miss it and you may fall back to the base coverage. Ask your installer whether they handle registration, keep the paperwork, and note the model and serial numbers of every indoor and outdoor unit.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Installation method affects coverage. Many brands limit or void warranty coverage on systems not installed by a licensed contractor, while DIY-marketed systems with pre-charged quick-connect line sets are designed for owner installation and carry their own terms. If you are tempted to install a conventional system yourself to save money, check the warranty language first, because a compressor failure on an uncovered system can wipe out the savings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Refrigerant rules are worth knowing, too. Under the EPA's rules implementing the AIM Act, new residential air conditioning and heat pump equipment is moving away from R-410A to lower global warming potential refrigerants such as R-32 and R-454B. Existing R-410A systems can still be serviced, but supply of that refrigerant is being phased down over time, which may affect repair costs on older systems in the years ahead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Should You Repair or Replace a Mini Split?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use age, warranty and the failed part together. Sensors, fan motors, drain problems and leaks at flare joints are usually worth fixing at almost any age. An inverter board under warranty is a repair. An out-of-warranty compressor on a system past ten or twelve years usually is not, because by the time you pay for the part and the labor, you are a large fraction of the way toward a new, more efficient system with a fresh warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multi-zone systems complicate the math. One outdoor unit serving three or four indoor heads means a compressor failure takes every zone down at once, and replacing it can be expensive. It also means the outdoor unit and heads must be compatible, so replacing only part of an aging multi-zone system is not always possible. Single-zone systems are easier to replace one room at a time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Efficiency is a real factor on old systems. Mini split efficiency has improved, particularly in cold-climate heating performance, so a twelve-year-old system may cost noticeably more to run than a current model, especially if it is your primary heat. That does not justify replacing a healthy system on its own, but it should weigh into the decision when a big repair arrives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look at the whole house. A mini split is one of several big systems with a lifespan clock, along with the water heater, sump pump and appliances. If the timing works, coordinating replacements can avoid surprise emergencies. Our guides to <a href="/how-long-do-sump-pumps-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long sump pumps last</a> and <a href="/how-long-do-tankless-water-heaters-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long tankless water heaters last</a> cover two more of those clocks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Topping up refrigerant without fixing a leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mini split is a sealed system, so low refrigerant always means a leak. Adding refrigerant without finding the leak, often at a flare joint, means you will be paying for another top-up later while the compressor runs short of charge. Insist that the technician locates and repairs the leak, then pressure tests and evacuates properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the filters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Washable filters are easy to forget because they are hidden behind the front panel. Clogged filters reduce airflow, make the system work harder, can cause icing in cooling mode and let dust coat the coil and blower wheel. Many manuals suggest cleaning every two to four weeks in heavy use; set a phone reminder and actually do it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hiring the cheapest installer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mini split lifespan is largely set on installation day. Flare quality, torque, nitrogen pressure testing and a deep vacuum all matter, and they are invisible once the job is done. A low bid that skips steps can cost you a compressor years later. Ask installers how they test and evacuate, and whether they register the warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure washing the outdoor coil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The aluminum fins on an outdoor coil are thin and bend easily. A pressure washer can flatten them and block airflow, which reduces efficiency and strains the system. Use a garden hose with gentle pressure, spraying from the inside out where the manual allows, and use a fin comb to straighten bent fins carefully.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping warranty registration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many brands give their longest coverage only to systems registered within a set number of days after installation. Miss that window and you may lose years of parts and compressor coverage. Ask your installer whether they register it, confirm it yourself on the manufacturer's site, and keep the paperwork with the model and serial numbers.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do mini splits last longer than central air conditioners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are in a similar range, roughly 12 to 20 years for mini splits and about 15 years for central systems. Mini splits often run more hours because they heat and cool, which adds wear, but inverter compressors avoid the hard on-off cycling of single-speed units. Installation quality and maintenance matter more than the system type.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a mini split be professionally serviced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An annual check is a sensible default, especially for systems used year-round. A technician can inspect electrical connections, check operation and refrigerant pressures, clear drains and assess whether the indoor coils and blower wheels need deep cleaning. Owners should handle filter cleaning themselves between visits, typically every few weeks during heavy use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my mini split smell musty when it starts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A musty or dirty sock smell usually means mold or bacteria growing on the indoor coil, blower wheel or drain pan, where moisture and dust collect. Clean the filters first. If the smell persists, the head likely needs a professional deep cleaning. Running the fan or a dry mode after cooling can help reduce moisture inside the unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a mini split last 25 years?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is possible but not typical. A quality system installed carefully, in a mild climate away from salt air, with diligent filter cleaning and periodic professional cleaning, can run past twenty years. Most owners should plan around fifteen, because inverter boards, fan motors and compressors eventually wear and refrigerant availability for older systems may change.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it normal for a mini split outdoor unit to ice up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light frost in heating mode is normal, and the system periodically runs a defrost cycle to clear it, sometimes with steam and a pause in heating. Heavy ice that does not clear, ice building up from below, or icing in cooling mode is not normal and can point to airflow problems, low refrigerant, drainage issues or a failed sensor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a DIY mini split last as long as a professionally installed one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A DIY-marketed system with pre-charged quick-connect lines is designed to be installed by owners and can perform well when installed carefully. The risks are leaks at connections, poor placement and drainage, and electrical mistakes. Conventional systems installed without proper vacuum and pressure testing are more likely to have problems, and may lose warranty coverage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most expensive mini split repair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Compressor replacement is generally the most expensive repair, followed by outdoor inverter board replacement. Both often require refrigerant recovery, evacuation and recharging, which adds significant labor. On an older system out of warranty, a compressor failure usually tips the decision toward replacement, especially on multi-zone systems where one outdoor unit serves every room.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

